{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Then I Myself will set My face, etc.</b> The plain sense of the verse indicates that if the people do not execute judgment, HaShem will execute judgment upon him and upon his family as well — something that would not happen if the people of the land had executed judgment. Yet these are far-fetched outcomes: whichever way you look at it, if they are guilty, whether the people executed judgment or not, the wicked man himself [should be punished in either case]." "<b>Now it appears</b> to explain it along this path: <b>And if the people of the land hide their eyes, etc.</b> — meaning, in a place where there is no judgment below, there will be judgment above, ״Then I Myself will set My face,״ etc. And when judgment is executed above, the registers of his family members will also be opened, and the charges will be stirred up against the entire family; this is what it says: <b>and his family.</b> He then returned to explain what this setting of the face does, and said: concerning the man himself — ״I will cut him off״; and concerning his family — their record-books will be opened; if among them are those who whore after [idols] like him, they are included in the excision, and this is what it says: ״and all who whore after him״ — of his family. But those whose books were opened and who are found not to have whored will not be cut off. Furthermore, he said <b>to whore</b> and did not suffice with saying ״those who whore, etc., after the Molech״ — this intends to say that even if one did not whore until now, meaning he did not pass his child to the Molech, but merely thought to whore — a thought of idolatry is seized upon as though it were a deed, and HaShem will cut off the one who thinks as though he were the one who acts, as the verse says (Ezekiel 14:5): ״לְמַעַן תְּפֹשׂ אֶת בֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּלִבָּם״." "<b>You thus find</b> that if the people of the land execute judgment against the evildoer — there is no judgment above, and no one of Israel will die other than the one who serves the Molech. But if they do not execute judgment, the books of the entire family are opened, and the charges are stirred up against them as well, and they are seized even for [mere] thought. And this is the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Deuteronomy Rabbah 5:4): 'In a place where there is judgment, etc., there is not, etc.' — end of quote. It is difficult: it is obvious that when they execute judgment below, what place is there for judgment to be carried out above? But according to our approach it is precisely explained: when judgment is executed below, the judgment above is prevented — meaning, the judgment of the rest, whose books are not opened. And you will find that our Sages of blessed memory said (Rosh HaShanah 16b) that when the books of judgment are opened, a person enters into danger and needs great merit to escape."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:58.810149562Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}